CHALLENGES ENCOUNTERED

One challenge was keeping the Tkinter Canvas and the Pillow image synchronized. Drawing directly on the Canvas would look correct on screen but would not be saved. This was solved by drawing on the Pillow image first and then refreshing the Canvas from that image after every change.

Another challenge was making Undo reverse a whole stroke instead of every small mouse movement. This was solved by saving one image snapshot when the mouse button is pressed and not saving again until the next complete action begins.

A third challenge was showing a shape preview without permanently changing the artwork. This was solved by drawing the preview only on the Canvas with a special preview tag, then deleting it and drawing the final shape on the Pillow image when the mouse button is released.

Fitting opened images into the fixed canvas was also tricky. The solution was to resize the image proportionally and paste it onto a white background that is exactly the canvas size, so the saved image always matches the visible canvas.

CONCLUSION

This project helped me understand how a graphical application combines interface events with real image data. I learned how mouse events control drawing, how Pillow stores and modifies images, and why it is important to keep the displayed view and the saved file identical. Building the undo history and shape previews also improved my understanding of organizing code into clear, reusable functions.
